    "result_text": "The full suite is green (the project's test surface here is the single acceptance test). \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `timezone` field derived from the owner's locality/region via a fixed region-to-timezone table.\n\nFollowing the repo's existing lookup-table pattern (`CityRegionTable`, `RegionPostcodeTable`), I:\n\n1. **Created `RegionTimezoneTable`** (`model/RegionTimezoneTable.java`) \u2014 the single source of truth mapping region \u2192 IANA timezone (NSW\u2192Australia/Sydney, VIC\u2192Australia/Melbourne, QLD\u2192Australia/Brisbane), returning `null` for regions with no known timezone (e.g. `UNKNOWN`).\n\n2. **Added a derived `getTimezone()` to `Owner`** \u2014 a `@Transient` getter that resolves the timezone from `getLocality()` via the table, mirroring how `getLocality()`, `getContactPreference()`, etc. are derived and kept consistent with the owner's own fields.\n\n3. **Added the `timezone` schema field** to `openapi.yml` (readOnly, alongside `locality`), so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it and MapStruct auto-maps `Owner.getTimezone()` \u2192 `OwnerDto.timezone` by name \u2014 no mapper changes needed.\n\nEach piece sits in its natural home: the table owns the mapping, `Owner` owns the derivation, and the DTO/mapper wiring falls out of the existing name-matching convention.",
